Z||000000||bHooper||03-02-2001||05:00 PM||||while driving around today, and listening to my quiet exhaust system (after replacing both manifolds), i was thinking about all those wonderfull sportscars of the '60's and 70's, with their organ pipe weber's... what a musical sound those cars made!<P>now my question: can we install this kind of carb. system on a fiero?  will it offer any performance gains?  lets say, i was going to do a 3.4 or a 4.9 liter, and wanted to be really unique... could i do this?  will it work with the computer? <P>if someone was to build them a really nice performance engine (maybe not the fanciest thing but nice), do you need to have a computer system in the car?<P>just curious.<P>thanks!<BR>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000001||GT GT 87||03-02-2001||06:37 PM||||Carberators don"t need computers but it would be needed for fuel pump,etc.  I wonder If anyone has any experience with the "off road"  intake manifold that is in GM's Hi-Perf. catalog?????It's for 2.8 60* engines.  It would be cool to get rid of 90% of all those wires in engine compartment!!!Could it make the same power?  Steve||||reg||1
Z||000002||rogergarrison||03-02-2001||07:25 PM||||before I finally opted for the 3.1 w/turbo, I was toying with the idea of using archies kit, but using a 64-67 Corvette 327, 375 hp 2 4bbl. I had a line on one from friends in local vette club. No computers, no FI, just a reg elec fuel pump HEI distr, and all the kick azz power that I could use.||||reg||1
Z||000003||gabriel||03-02-2001||07:38 PM||||&gt;No computers, no FI, just a reg elec fuel pump HEI distr, and all the kick azz power that I could use.<P>  I had thought of the same thing (not a rat motor, though) but can you make a 60s - 70s carbed mill pass emissions?||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||03-02-2001||08:41 PM||||now i dont understand all the terminology and technology that goes into cars and their computers, but b4 about 18 years ago not many cars had computers.  so, i'm guessing/assuming that they are mostly for emisions and such, right?  and yes, they program them to run the injectors and fuel pump, but if you were to build an engine, youi wouldn't necessarily need all that, right?<P>computers+vacuum lines=emisions equipment<P>is that a pretty safe formula?<BR><P>------------------<BR>hoop<BR>Red '86 GT<BR>KYB's, Eibach's,<BR>Poly Bushings||||reg||1
Z||000005||hugh||03-02-2001||08:53 PM||||Computers are the big difference in the cars of today and yesterday.They cut emmissions,increase power to a level that 40 years ago couldn't be dreamed about.I have a GN and get over 20 mpg,have well over 400 horsepower and can run faster in the 1/4 now with the air conditioner on than the hottest factory race cars of the late 50's and early 60's.All this with a 3.8 v6.||||reg||1
Z||000006||ray b||03-02-2001||09:19 PM||||webers are very adjustable but need custom<BR>manafold, would look sweet on a northstar.<BR>you could tune it to pass EPA but would run like a dog.but you don't have to leave it that way just retune it after.<BR>cost will be very high and hard to find parts.FI was used in racecars in 60's as it makes more power than the best carbs.||||reg||1
Z||000007||bubbajoexx||03-02-2001||11:34 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by GT GT 87:<BR><B>Carberators don"t need computers but it would be needed for fuel pump,etc.  I wonder If anyone has any experience with the "off road"  intake manifold that is in GM's Hi-Perf. catalog?????It's for 2.8 60* engines.  It would be cool to get rid of 90% of all those wires in engine compartment!!!Could it make the same power?  Steve</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>if you go with a carb fuel system you do not need any computer wat so ever the fuel pump works of oil pressure and you can triger the relay of the crank output wire to start the pump durring startup untill you oil pressure comes up also I just built a 4.9 with a carb and hei ignition no computer I will Post a couple of pics tomorrow for you to see<p>[This message has been edited by bubbajoexx (edited 03-02-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000008||gabriel||03-03-2001||12:39 AM||||&gt;They cut emmissions,increase power to a level that 40 years ago couldn't be dreamed about.I have a GN and get over 20 mpg,have well over 400 horsepower and can run faster in the 1/4 now with the air conditioner on than the hottest factory race cars of the late 50's and early 60's.All this with a 3.8 v6.<P>  Hugh, I ran a Ford 302 in the early 70s at Maple Grove that had nowhere near 400hp,  (try 350) a mustang that was nowhere near as light as a Gn, and still broke the 12s.  As well as a few parts ...<P> 400hp?   I've grown leary of anyone claiming that figure ...<P>  Not saying you don't have it ...   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  - so don't get mad at me, but with the 3800 series II Supercharged(unmodified) dynoing ~ 220 -240 in real life & 300 w/ mods ................||||reg||1
Z||000009||Cooter||03-03-2001||02:02 AM||||Each year, I read the articles about the GN vs. Mustang shootout. The little v6 3.8 is capable of putting out up to 1000 H/P before the crank gives up and the 302 lives happier when it does not get more than 750 H/P. 400 horses out of a 3.8 turbo is not only conservative, but also a long living combination.||||reg||1
Z||000010||hugh||03-03-2001||06:40 AM||||The GN weighs over 3500 lbs.according to Buick,with me in it ,it has to weigh almost 3700lbs.I believe the mustangs weighed around 3200lbs.I ran 12.20 with a 1.95 60 foot time due to the fact that all I did was spin the 10 inch slicks all through 1st and 2nd gears.It was my fault,as the car was too low in the front and had no weight transfer.My speed was just under 115mph which computes to approximately 441 horsepower. I haven't raced the car much since then(no time)But I have since installed a larger turbo,replaced the stock headers,put in a 9" Ford rear with 3.89 gears(ran that ET and speed with the stock 3.43 rear).My car had about 50,000 miles on it at that time and has never broken a part,though I replaced the rear because I didn't think it had much time left.My engine is at it's horsepower limit because the cylinders only have 4 headbolts each and can't contain the pressures.Two more things.This is my family car,I use it all the time.I do get over 20 mpg on the highway and 15 around town.And I don't get mad that easy,especially at people who may never have ridden in one of these cars to experience their awesome torque. ||||reg||1
Z||000011||gabriel||03-03-2001||08:06 AM||||&gt;And I don't get mad that easy,especially at people who may never have ridden in one of these cars to experience their awesome torque. <P>                 <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>  That's all good news, because I do have the 3800SC ...||||reg||1
Z||000012||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||10:10 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by bHooper:<BR><B>now i dont understand all the terminology and technology that goes into cars and their computers, but b4 about 18 years ago not many cars had computers.  so, i'm guessing/assuming that they are mostly for emisions and such, right?  and yes, they program them to run the injectors and fuel pump, but if you were to build an engine, youi wouldn't necessarily need all that, right?<P>computers+vacuum lines=emisions equipment<P>is that a pretty safe formula?<P><BR>the computer stuff is mostly for emmissions and increasing fuel milage. In ohio, I have no emmissions testing so thats not a prob. I dont know if youve ever drove one of those awsome 327's, but theyll melt the tires rite down to the rims if you wanted. The torque is tremendous. I had a 1964 Ply Valient years ago with a 426 cross ram Hemi. I could pull the front wheels a foot from a red lite and I drove it everyday to work. ran 11's when I was into drag racing before ET bracket racing started.<P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>||||reg||1
Z||000013||gabriel||03-03-2001||12:37 PM||||&gt;. I had a 1964 Ply Valient years ago with a 426 cross ram Hemi. I could pull the front wheels a foot from a red lite and I drove it everyday to work. ran 11's when I was into drag racing before ET bracket racing started.<P>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Did that Valient have the Hemi option or did you do that yourself?   I thought the cudas were the only ones that you could get the hemi in at the time.<BR>  The Hemi WAS bad!!<P>||||reg||1
