Z||000015||rogergarrison||03-03-2001||02:52 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by gabriel:<BR><B>&gt;. I had a 1964 Ply Valient years ago with a 426 cross ram Hemi. I could pull the front wheels a foot from a red lite and I drove it everyday to work. ran 11's when I was into drag racing before ET bracket racing started.<P>     <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif">   Did that Valient have the Hemi option or did you do that yourself?   I thought the cudas were the only ones that you could get the hemi in at the time.<BR>  The Hemi WAS bad!!<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>No it was a 235 slant 6 automatic. We pulled the 727 tranny and hemi out of a tornado wrecked brand new Dodge SuperBee.<P>thats a whole nether story. back in 69 or 70 a big tornado hit Oklahoma City where I was stationed in the AF. The tornado demolished a new Chrysler/Dodge/Plymouth Dealership. All the cars were totaled on the lot and to clear out the area they sold all the cars first come first serve for $200.00 each with no title/papers, just bill of sale. I got a brand new Super Bee blue/white top and interior, with like 7 miles on odom. My friend got an orange RoadRunner. both had the roofs, hoods and trunks smashed in from collapsing roof. Talk about a deal, LOL<P><p>[This message has been edited by rogergarrison (edited 03-03-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000016||hugh||03-04-2001||12:53 PM||||I should have said in my previous post that my brother's car was a 1987 T-Type with a 274 inch engine built by Ruggles in Georgia.We had all kind of problems with the TEC2 system he used.We couldn't keep it fueled through the whole 1/4 mile because of the programing we got from the factory.We were changing injectors and reprograming the pulsewidth every week and could not solve the problem.The only good run he had with it ,he overfueled it so much he had trouble keeping it running at the line.It popped a few times down the strip but ran an 11.09 at 120 mph through the mufflers.That car had the pontential to run low 10's at close to 130 mph and was street driven.We drove to Reading with it for the nationals.He would still have it,except with all the fueling problems it had and all the preigniton it suffered due to fueling he hurt the crank.The car was returned to stock and sold.||||reg||1
Z||000017||lowCG||03-04-2001||01:39 PM||||I've always been a fan of all that euro performance stuff,but after lving with it for twenty years,I can say that it's not a bad time to move on.<BR>A good way to get the same effect as a set of weber trumpets would be to use an aftermarket fuel injection controller from Simple Digital Systems and a custom intake,thought about doing that on my TDC,but it runs and sounds pretty neat just the way it is.||||reg||1
